{"authored" [{"question" "q-33063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ at the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee9130129198009e4aeb1db06c341c9531956ca" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(2\\pi ).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =i+0\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =i+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+0+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2\\pi ,1,0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee9130129198009e4aeb1db06c341c9531956ca" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b896077b3f61a65625294d246f92b173da2405f8e0fe554beffd6b1050162333" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee9130129198009e4aeb1db06c341c9531956ca" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee