_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee9130129198009e4aeb1db06c341c9531956ca" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee9130129198009e4aeb1db06c341c9531956ca" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/state.json" "file_sha256" "50cd5ab5d18e05379074b5418ee9130129198009e4aeb1db06c341c9531956ca" "basis" 2829 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14079396/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-33063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$ at the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by \n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k & =2\\pi \\,i+\\,j+0\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2\\pi  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2\\pi ,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(2\\pi ).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2\\pi :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2\\pi ) & =\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =i+0\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =i+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+0+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2\\pi )$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2\\pi ,1,0):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2\\pi ) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2\\pi )}{‖{r}^{′}(2\\pi )‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot (i+k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b21fd975757de8a754dc61c265058cc067d03f49db44cd60604ccc15bf5731a"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,j"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33063" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k$ at the point $P(2,4,8)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,j+y(t)\\,j+z(t)\\,k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,4,8)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\,k & =2\\,i+4\\,j+8\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=4 \\\\ 2{t}^{2}=8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(2,4,8)$ is at the tip of $r(2).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+4t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =\\,i+2\\cdot 2\\,j+4\\cdot 2\\,k \\\\  & =i+4\\,j+8\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(2)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{8}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{81} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(2)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(2,4,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(2)}{‖{r}^{′}(2)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (i+4\\,j+8\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33130" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b21fd975757de8a754dc61c265058cc067d03f49db44cd60604ccc15bf5731a"} {"question" "q-33130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{8}{9}\\,j+\\frac{4}{9}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{21}\\,i+\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}\\,j+\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{69}}{69}\\,i+\\frac{2\\sqrt{69}}{69}\\,j+\\frac{8\\sqrt{69}}{69}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33130" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{4}{9}\\,j+\\frac{8}{9}\\,k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point $P(4,0,1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),y(t),z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(4,0,1)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,{t}^{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4t=4 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, the point $P(4,0,1)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨4,\\,\\frac{4}{(1)},\\,2(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the given curve at $P(4,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}⟨4,\\,4,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b21fd975757de8a754dc61c265058cc067d03f49db44cd60604ccc15bf5731a"} {"question" "q-33135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{2}{5}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩"] ["math" "⟨\\frac{2}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{5},\\,\\frac{1}{5}⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-33135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-64492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)={t}^{2}p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=1$, given that  $p(1)=i-\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(1)=-i+\\,j+\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-64492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\cdot p(t)+{t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+{t}^{2}\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =2(1)\\,p(1)+(1)^{2}\\,{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2(i-\\,j-k)+(-i+\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =2i-2\\,j-2k-i+j+\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=1$ is  $T(1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-64492" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b21fd975757de8a754dc61c265058cc067d03f49db44cd60604ccc15bf5731a"} {"question" "q-64492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-64492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}(i+j-k)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}(i-j-k)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(i+j-2k)"] ["math" "\\sqrt{3}(i+j-k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-64492" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2t)p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=1$, given that  $p(1)=i+2\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(1)=2\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}-2t)p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot p(t)+({t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =(2t-2)\\,p(t)+({t}^{2}-2t)\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =(2(1)-2)\\,p(1)+((1)^{2}-2(1))\\,{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =0-{p}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =-(2\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k) \\\\  & =-2i+\\,j+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(1)}{‖{r}^{′}(1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-2\\,i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=1$ is  $T(1)=-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7b21fd975757de8a754dc61c265058cc067d03f49db44cd60604ccc15bf5731a"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14080906/state.json" "file_sha256" "d38fcc30a6bd61f4429e9fef60a7d654f18c8b39947c191472c4f22a0ead9260"} {"question" "q-75228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k"] ["math" "-\\frac{1}{9}\\,i+\\frac{1}{9}\\,j+\\frac{2}{9}\\,k"] ["math" "-\\frac{2}{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{3}\\,j+\\frac{2}{3}\\,k"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(-2\\,i+\\,j+2\\,k)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(2\\,i-\\,j